WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.880
The first axons start out through the far end of a growing leg.

2
00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.840
No earlier axons lie ahead of them.

3
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:11.640
How do they find the way?

4
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.740
The moving tip of a growing axon is the growth cone.

5
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:17.720
It does not carry a map.

6
00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:22.710
It is roughly 10 µm across, and its filopodia reach farther.

7
00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.270
It can touch what lies against it.

8
00:00:26.183 --> 00:00:29.223
And it can compare how much of a cue reaches its two sides.

9
00:00:30.267 --> 00:00:31.727
Take the simplest case.

10
00:00:32.587 --> 00:00:35.647
One cell releases an attractant into uniform tissue.

11
00:00:36.507 --> 00:00:38.347
The growth cone heads straight up its gradient.

12
00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:40.807
What shape is the path?

13
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.260
straight line to the source.

14
00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:52.820
In this ideal case, the cue falls off equally in every direction.

15
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.840
So uphill always points at the source.

16
00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.140
And at the source, the climb ends.

17
00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:01.573
Add a second source, and the path can curve.

18
00:01:02.393 --> 00:01:04.213
Where it ends depends on where it starts.

19
00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:09.147
Either way, an attractant gives a destination, not a list of stops.

20
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.160
Real routes reach a place and go on from it.

21
00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:19.480
The first axons out of an insect leg make several sharp turns.

22
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:24.840
A turn like that usually means the growth cone has started reading something new.

23
00:01:25.833 --> 00:01:29.153
A cell it touches, a boundary in the tissue, or a different cue.

24
00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:31.847
Reach adds a second limit.

25
00:01:32.687 --> 00:01:34.387
A secreted cue thins as it spreads.

26
00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:39.530
Far enough away, the difference across a growth cone is lost in noise.

27
00:01:40.583 --> 00:01:45.543
Simple models with plausible values put that at hundreds of micrometres to a few

28
00:01:45.543 --> 00:01:48.783
millimetres. It is an estimate, not a fixed range.

29
00:01:49.817 --> 00:01:51.037
So here is the rule.

30
00:01:51.917 --> 00:01:54.257
A growth cone reads only what is within its reach.

31
00:01:55.267 --> 00:01:58.247
So a long route is usually built as a chain of legs.

32
00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:03.447
Each leg ends at a choice point, where the growth cone picks its next path.

33
00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:07.107
There it reads something new, or reads the same thing differently.

34
00:02:08.217 --> 00:02:12.257
At many choice points, growth cones pause and spread out before they choose.

35
00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:15.307
Not every bend needs a new reading.

36
00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:19.187
A pioneer axon can lead followers around long curves.
